<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jokhang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Jokhang Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jokhang is the most sacred temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, traditionally founded in the seventh century during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>. It houses the Jowo, a statue of the Buddha said to have been brought by the Tang princess Wencheng, and pilgrims circle it on the Barkhor. The pillar bearing the treaty of 821–822 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the Tang stands before it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jokhang.jpg" alt="Jokhang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: onwardtibet.org · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jokhang_Temple_in_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
